  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nCurrently, an upper trough is sliding across the northern Plains \nwith a shortwave trough ejecting out of the Rockies into the central \nPlains. Upper ridging has started building into the western CONUS. \nAt the surface, a low is over northern Missouri with a cold front \ndraping down through southeast Kansas into northwestern Oklahoma and \nwestern Texas. Showers are starting to taper off in southeast Kansas \nwith chances continuing to decrease into this evening. As we \ncontinue into tonight, colder air will move in with lows dropping \ninto the upper 30s and lower 40s on early Friday morning. \n\nFor Friday, the colder airmass with falling heights/decreased \nthickness will settle in, resulting in high temperatures topping out \nin the low 50s across the area. This is about 15 to 20 degrees below \nnormal making this the coldest fall temperatures we have seen so \nfar. As the upper trough and associated surface low propagate east \nthroughout the day Friday, an increased pressure gradient will \nremain over eastern Kansas. This will bring breezy conditions to the \narea with sustained winds between 20-25 mph and gusts up to 35 mph. \nUpper ridging will build into the Plains on Friday night, shifting a \nsurface high to over Kansas. Winds will calm significantly into \nSaturday morning with lows dropping into the lower 30s, likely \nresulting in widespread frost across the area except potentially for \nfar southeast Kansas where lows in the mid to upper 30s are \npossible. Lows near 30 are likely in central KS, so hoisted a Freeze \nWatch for this potential. Cooler conditions will persist into \nSaturday as expansive high pressure remains over eastern Kansas with \ndecreased thickness. High temperatures will be in the upper 50s to \nnear 60 on Saturday afternoon with lows dropping into the upper 30s \non early Sunday morning. \n\nOn Sunday, an upper shortwave and its associated surface low will \nslide across southern Canada with surface troughing extending into \nthe central Rockies, increasing the pressure gradient across Kansas. \nBreezy conditions will return especially for areas west of the \nKansas Turnpike. Sustained winds are forecast between 15 to 25 mph \nwith gusts up to 30 mph with the strongest winds expected in central \nKS. High temperatures will return to the 60s on Sunday afternoon \nwith lows in the mid 40s early Monday morning. Guidance suggests a \nlow chance of showers late Sunday night into Monday, though \nconfidence is low so kept mention of this out at this time.  \n\nFor Monday and beyond, upper ridging will continue to influence the \nPlains, promoting a warming trend Monday through Thursday. Highs \nwill return to the 70s with readings near 80 on both Wednesday and \nThursday. The next chance for showers and storms will potentially \narrive mid to late next week as a deep upper trough over the western \nCONUS will traverse towards the Rockies on Thursday, though there is \nstill uncertainty. Stay tuned as we continue to refine this \nforecast.",
